WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.390 --> 00:00:01.500
- [Narrator] The U.S. and China,

00:00:01.500 --> 00:00:04.560
are two of the world's
biggest cotton producers.

00:00:04.560 --> 00:00:06.660
The U.S. exports most of its cotton

00:00:06.660 --> 00:00:09.390
and its biggest customer is China.

00:00:09.390 --> 00:00:13.260
Which bought nearly $3 billion
of American cotton last year.

00:00:13.260 --> 00:00:16.230
At the same time, China
exports the largest share

00:00:16.230 --> 00:00:18.750
of its finished textiles to the U.S.,

00:00:18.750 --> 00:00:21.900
but the balance of that
relationship is shifting.

00:00:21.900 --> 00:00:23.940
In 2021, president Joe Biden

00:00:23.940 --> 00:00:25.740
signed a law banning imports from China's

00:00:25.740 --> 00:00:27.840
main cotton growing region Xinjiang.

00:00:27.840 --> 00:00:29.130
Home of millions of Uyghurs

00:00:29.130 --> 00:00:30.930
and other ethnic minorities.

00:00:30.930 --> 00:00:32.670
Human rights groups have accused China

00:00:32.670 --> 00:00:34.800
of detaining as many as 1 million people

00:00:34.800 --> 00:00:36.390
and forcing them into labor,

00:00:36.390 --> 00:00:38.760
which China has repeatedly denied

00:00:38.760 --> 00:00:40.800
- The impact of banning this

00:00:40.800 --> 00:00:44.160
cotton in China can't be overstated.

00:00:44.160 --> 00:00:45.600
- [Narrator] U.S. cotton prices surged,

00:00:45.600 --> 00:00:46.890
after many American companies

00:00:46.890 --> 00:00:48.960
scrambled for new cotton sources.

00:00:48.960 --> 00:00:50.490
China even started importing

00:00:50.490 --> 00:00:52.080
more American cotton

00:00:52.080 --> 00:00:53.970
since lots of its domestic supply,

00:00:53.970 --> 00:00:56.760
couldn't be used for
goods sent to the U.S..

00:00:56.760 --> 00:00:59.400
But now those imports have been declining.

00:00:59.400 --> 00:01:01.470
This comes at a time
when U.S. cotton farmers

00:01:01.470 --> 00:01:02.520
were already struggling

00:01:02.520 --> 00:01:04.650
due to lower prices and drought.

00:01:04.650 --> 00:01:08.040
And because China is so enmeshed
in the cotton supply chain

00:01:08.040 --> 00:01:09.360
plenty of Xinjiang cotton

00:01:09.360 --> 00:01:11.430
is still making it into the U.S.,

00:01:11.430 --> 00:01:12.530
according to companies

00:01:13.396 --> 00:01:15.060
that do forensic testing
of cotton apparel.

00:01:15.060 --> 00:01:17.760
Here's how the U.S. effort
to stamp out forced labor

00:01:17.760 --> 00:01:20.553
has impacted the cotton
market in both countries.

00:01:21.540 --> 00:01:24.420
The U.S. grows about 12%
of the world's cotton.

00:01:24.420 --> 00:01:28.500
China grows more than
double that, about 26%.

00:01:28.500 --> 00:01:30.242
And China isn't just

00:01:30.242 --> 00:01:32.411
the world's biggest grower of cotton,

00:01:32.411 --> 00:01:34.080
it's a key processor of it too.

00:01:34.080 --> 00:01:37.320
- The garment processing
textile processing industry

00:01:37.320 --> 00:01:40.410
really isn't based that much
in the United States anymore.

00:01:40.410 --> 00:01:44.250
So it goes overseas China
is a big destination

00:01:44.250 --> 00:01:47.100
but then you also have Bangladesh,

00:01:47.100 --> 00:01:48.840
Vietnam, Sri Lanka.

00:01:48.840 --> 00:01:51.630
- [Narrator] According to
estimates from December, 2022,

00:01:51.630 --> 00:01:55.173
China milled about 35.5
million bales of cotton.

00:01:56.295 --> 00:01:59.010
Last year, the U.S. milled
about 2.3 million bales.

00:01:59.010 --> 00:02:00.480
China has remained a key player

00:02:00.480 --> 00:02:01.740
in the cotton supply chain

00:02:01.740 --> 00:02:03.780
but its role has been shifting.

00:02:03.780 --> 00:02:06.870
More and more other countries
like Vietnam, Pakistan

00:02:06.870 --> 00:02:09.720
and Bangladesh have been
importing raw cotton,

00:02:09.720 --> 00:02:10.983
milling it into yarn,

00:02:11.910 --> 00:02:12.870
and then sending the yarn to China.

00:02:12.870 --> 00:02:14.969
- It is kind of tough to know

00:02:14.969 --> 00:02:19.770
where exactly your cotton is coming from.

00:02:19.770 --> 00:02:21.840
I think the way that a lot
of these facilities work

00:02:21.840 --> 00:02:24.000
it's really tough to audit whether or not,

00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:25.230
a Chinese mill, for example,

00:02:25.230 --> 00:02:27.780
is using cotton that comes from Xinjiang

00:02:27.780 --> 00:02:29.880
or from elsewhere in the world.

00:02:29.880 --> 00:02:31.050
- [Narrator] As cotton gets cleaned,

00:02:31.050 --> 00:02:32.310
purified and finished

00:02:32.310 --> 00:02:34.260
via a complicated supply chain,

00:02:34.260 --> 00:02:36.744
it becomes harder and harder to know

00:02:36.744 --> 00:02:37.890
where the original cotton came from,

00:02:37.890 --> 00:02:39.060
which makes it easier

00:02:39.060 --> 00:02:42.090
for companies to dodge
import restrictions.

00:02:42.090 --> 00:02:44.490
Then there's the final
stage of the process,

00:02:44.490 --> 00:02:47.370
using yarn or fabric to
make the finished goods.

00:02:47.370 --> 00:02:49.590
China also leads here.

00:02:49.590 --> 00:02:51.210
U.S. trade statistics suggest

00:02:51.210 --> 00:02:53.964
U.S. imports of cotton products from China

00:02:53.964 --> 00:02:55.140
have decreased over the last decade.

00:02:55.140 --> 00:02:56.940
Though the country is
still the biggest source

00:02:56.940 --> 00:02:59.370
of U.S. cotton textiles and apparel.

00:02:59.370 --> 00:03:02.520
The U.S. meanwhile makes few
of its own finished goods.

00:03:02.520 --> 00:03:05.070
One trade group found
less than 3% of apparel

00:03:05.070 --> 00:03:08.100
sold in the U.S. last year
was produced domestically.

00:03:08.100 --> 00:03:10.260
Though those figures
weren't specific to cotton.

00:03:10.260 --> 00:03:13.590
- We live in a hyper globally
competitive marketplace.

00:03:13.590 --> 00:03:15.270
You've seen a decimation

00:03:15.270 --> 00:03:17.580
of critical aspects of our industry.

00:03:17.580 --> 00:03:19.650
- [Narrator] Kim Glas
is the president and CEO

00:03:19.650 --> 00:03:22.350
of the National Council
of Textile Organizations,

00:03:22.350 --> 00:03:24.840
which represents domestic manufacturers.

00:03:24.840 --> 00:03:27.060
She says goods made by
forced labor in China

00:03:27.060 --> 00:03:30.210
make it impossible for
American businesses to compete.

00:03:30.210 --> 00:03:32.280
- In the cotton supply chain,

00:03:32.280 --> 00:03:34.440
it's pennies that moves

00:03:34.440 --> 00:03:36.330
and shifts production globally

00:03:36.330 --> 00:03:38.430
from one country to another.

00:03:38.430 --> 00:03:41.425
And particularly on the areas of Xinjiang

00:03:41.425 --> 00:03:43.860
and Forced labor associated

00:03:43.860 --> 00:03:45.540
with those cotton supply chains

00:03:45.540 --> 00:03:48.090
that has had a severe economic impact

00:03:48.090 --> 00:03:50.610
on the U.S. domestic textile industry.

00:03:50.610 --> 00:03:52.230
- [Narrator] Importers have
to document the products

00:03:52.230 --> 00:03:53.790
they're bringing into the U.S.,

00:03:53.790 --> 00:03:56.310
but this doesn't always
tell the whole story.

00:03:56.310 --> 00:03:58.140
U.S. Customs and Border Protection

00:03:58.140 --> 00:04:00.660
detains some goods that
its risk assessment tools

00:04:00.660 --> 00:04:02.580
flag as potentially illegal,

00:04:02.580 --> 00:04:04.440
and importers are required to present

00:04:04.440 --> 00:04:07.110
additional documentation
to get them released.

00:04:07.110 --> 00:04:09.480
But some like Glas, say enforcement

00:04:09.480 --> 00:04:11.700
of the ban has been insufficient.

00:04:11.700 --> 00:04:13.830
- The numbers are completely underwhelming

00:04:13.830 --> 00:04:15.450
on enforcement activities.

00:04:15.450 --> 00:04:18.210
A few dozen shipments stopped per month.

00:04:18.210 --> 00:04:20.820
We're talking about thousands

00:04:20.820 --> 00:04:23.310
and millions of packages coming in

00:04:23.310 --> 00:04:25.950
that contain apparel items.

00:04:25.950 --> 00:04:27.870
We should be seeing stop shipments

00:04:27.870 --> 00:04:31.260
off the port of LA, every day.

00:04:31.260 --> 00:04:32.340
- [Narrator] The clearest way to know

00:04:32.340 --> 00:04:33.510
where an item comes from,

00:04:33.510 --> 00:04:36.570
is to test its chemical
components and DNA.

00:04:36.570 --> 00:04:38.490
Customs test some imports,

00:04:38.490 --> 00:04:40.200
but many companies also do their own

00:04:40.200 --> 00:04:42.240
to validate their supply chains.

00:04:42.240 --> 00:04:44.640
- Cotton is a plant and it has to,

00:04:44.640 --> 00:04:47.310
just like you and I, take in nutrients,

00:04:47.310 --> 00:04:51.000
so you can analyze those nutrients

00:04:51.000 --> 00:04:55.440
to get an idea of where
the plant was grown.

00:04:55.440 --> 00:04:56.910
- [Narrator] Applied DNA Sciences

00:04:56.910 --> 00:04:59.099
is a biotech company based

00:04:59.099 --> 00:04:59.932
on Long Island.

00:04:59.932 --> 00:05:02.040
It specializes in supply chain tracing

00:05:02.040 --> 00:05:04.740
as well as genetic
testing and therapeutics.

00:05:04.740 --> 00:05:07.170
- We have two different
colors of the same shirt

00:05:07.170 --> 00:05:09.420
submitted by the same client,

00:05:09.420 --> 00:05:11.820
both claimed to be from Brazil.

00:05:11.820 --> 00:05:15.840
One of them tested clearly
as being from Brazil

00:05:15.840 --> 00:05:18.150
and it matched with our
library Brazilian samples

00:05:18.150 --> 00:05:21.120
while the other one matched with Xinjiang.

00:05:21.120 --> 00:05:23.640
And there would be no
way on a rack to tell,

00:05:23.640 --> 00:05:25.863
they would be right next to each other

00:05:25.863 --> 00:05:26.696
a black and a white shirt.

00:05:26.696 --> 00:05:28.080
- [Narrator] They also do DNA tagging

00:05:28.080 --> 00:05:30.750
spraying a molecular tag onto raw fibers

00:05:30.750 --> 00:05:31.920
at the cotton gen,

00:05:31.920 --> 00:05:34.170
so companies can track their raw cotton

00:05:34.170 --> 00:05:35.420
through the supply chain.

00:05:36.572 --> 00:05:40.410
- About 14 or 15% of the finished foods

00:05:40.410 --> 00:05:45.410
we test show evidence of Xinjiang cotton.

00:05:45.630 --> 00:05:49.957
We tell the people who've
asked us to test that product

00:05:49.957 --> 00:05:54.957
and then they have to push
harder on their supply chain.

00:05:55.590 --> 00:05:57.519
- [Narrator] Given the complexity of

00:05:57.519 --> 00:05:58.935
ensuring goods from China

00:05:58.935 --> 00:06:00.635
don't contain Xinjiang cotton,

00:06:00.635 --> 00:06:01.860
retailers like L.L Bean and Mango

00:06:01.860 --> 00:06:03.750
have cut out China completely.

00:06:03.750 --> 00:06:06.660
- You see brands moving their operations

00:06:06.660 --> 00:06:08.486
to get around China in general,

00:06:08.486 --> 00:06:10.000
'cause they don't wanna
run into a situation

00:06:10.000 --> 00:06:11.580
where they can't sell in the U.S.

00:06:11.580 --> 00:06:13.770
You see them moving these companies

00:06:13.770 --> 00:06:15.840
moving their supply chains out of China

00:06:15.840 --> 00:06:20.070
to places like Vietnam,
or Bangladesh, Sri Lanka.

00:06:20.070 --> 00:06:21.720
You know all the names that you see

00:06:21.720 --> 00:06:23.340
when you look at the back of your shirt.

00:06:23.340 --> 00:06:25.518
- [Narrator] But this is missing

00:06:25.518 --> 00:06:26.351
a major part of the story.

00:06:26.351 --> 00:06:28.080
According to experts, many of these goods

00:06:28.080 --> 00:06:30.840
may have been processed
or even made in China

00:06:30.840 --> 00:06:34.170
but are being exported to
the U.S. via another country.

00:06:34.170 --> 00:06:35.790
Another thing, trade data misses

00:06:35.790 --> 00:06:37.950
is a loophole that many industry experts

00:06:37.950 --> 00:06:39.750
say allows China to ship goods

00:06:39.750 --> 00:06:42.390
containing banned cotton into the U.S..

00:06:42.390 --> 00:06:44.280
A huge number of shipments from China

00:06:44.280 --> 00:06:46.650
arrive through a rule called de minimis,

00:06:46.650 --> 00:06:48.990
which allows shipments worth under $800

00:06:48.990 --> 00:06:51.390
to come into the U.S.
without incurring tariffs

00:06:51.390 --> 00:06:53.610
or requiring as much paperwork.

00:06:53.610 --> 00:06:56.070
This is significantly
higher than the de minimis

00:06:56.070 --> 00:06:57.742
limit for imports to China,

00:06:57.742 --> 00:07:00.783
which is just 50 yuan, less than $7.

00:07:01.890 --> 00:07:04.290
Some lawmakers are trying
to modify this rule

00:07:04.290 --> 00:07:06.250
to keep ban Chinese cotton out

00:07:07.123 --> 00:07:08.970
and level the playing field
for U.S. cotton growers

00:07:08.970 --> 00:07:10.140
who have already been dealing

00:07:10.140 --> 00:07:12.660
with decreasing prices and drought.

00:07:12.660 --> 00:07:15.412
The Uyghur Forced Labor Protection Act

00:07:15.412 --> 00:07:18.030
is ultimately designed to
clamp down on forced labor

00:07:18.030 --> 00:07:22.080
but it also has major bearings
on the U.S. cotton industry.

00:07:22.080 --> 00:07:24.690
- Theoretically, when the
ban first came into place

00:07:24.690 --> 00:07:27.300
U.S. cotton growers were really hoping

00:07:27.300 --> 00:07:30.930
that that would give
cotton prices in general,

00:07:30.930 --> 00:07:33.330
a boost would give export demand a boost

00:07:33.330 --> 00:07:35.220
which it has to some extent.

00:07:35.220 --> 00:07:37.410
- [Narrator] But ultimately
it hasn't been enough,

00:07:37.410 --> 00:07:38.670
at least so far,

00:07:38.670 --> 00:07:40.410
to offset the long-term decline

00:07:40.410 --> 00:07:42.063
of the U.S. cotton industry.

00:07:43.355 --> 00:07:45.938
(upbeat music)

